diagnosis_text:
After a thorough examination of every layer of the path between Laptop2 and Laptop3, the network is fully healthy. Both hosts sit on switch1 — Laptop2 on port eth3 and Laptop3 on port eth4 — and both ports are correctly assigned to the same Linux bridge, `br_vlan10` (VLAN 10), in STP FORWARDING state with no errors. Laptop2 holds IP 172.16.10.12/24 and Laptop3 holds 172.16.10.13/24; they reside in the same /24 subnet, meaning traffic is switched entirely within switch1's `br_vlan10` bridge without needing to traverse the uplink to mswitch1. Direct ping tests in both directions returned 4/4 packets delivered with sub-3 ms latency, and ARP tables on both laptops show each other's MAC addresses correctly resolved. Packet filters (iptables and ebtables) have no rules and default ACCEPT policy. There are no traffic-control rate limiters or classifiers attached to the switch ports. Interface counters show zero RX/TX errors. The startup configuration was also inspected and correctly reflects the intended VLAN assignment. No fault exists at the switch, the port assignments, the spanning-tree state, or the IP configuration of either laptop. The user's reported inability to exchange files is not caused by any network-layer fault; the root cause is likely a transient disruption that has since cleared (such as a brief link flap), or an application-layer or OS-level issue on one of the laptops (e.g., a file-sharing service that failed to restart). Corrective action: verify the file-sharing daemon (Samba, NFS, etc.) is running on the target host and that the share path is exported correctly — no switch or cabling changes are needed.
